A||N||13||Taijiguy||LED current limiting question||||1||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||05-03-2001||10:03 AM||||The title pretty much says it. Can't find my little book with the info, and it's been a pretty long time, so I can't remember offhand. I'm making an LED brake light for my neighbors Harley. I'm making strings of 7-1.7v LEDs to bring the string operating voltage to 11.9v, but I can't remember how to limit the current on the strings. Can someone here remind me what it is I need to do to accomplish that? <BR>TIA<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||05-03-2001||12:16 PM||||Its been a while since I last studied/used any electonics coure I have taken, but:<P>current it proportional to voltage(I=V/R), and <BR>current is inversely proportional to resistance(R=V/I):<BR>I=V/R<BR>I=11.9/R<P>you just need to plug in the "R" value, and you have your "I" value". Its been a long time, and I don't remember  whole lot, but why do you need to limit current? You might want to install an inline fuse for possibilty of shorts later.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||05-03-2001||12:56 PM||||Well, the reason for needing current limiting devices is part of what's eluding me at the moment. In forward bias, diodes don't register any resistance. so these will light, but the current that passes throught them exceeds their capacity. They eventually heat up and self destruct. I <I>think</I> I just need to stick a resistor of some low value (like 33 ohms or so) in line with them just to keep the current from going crazy. I'm mostly just looking for confirmation on that. Or if there's a formula for determining what the resistor needs to be, have someone provide me with that.<BR>Thanks,<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||05-03-2001||01:07 PM||||If you know what the max current allowable is, you can use the R=V/I formula, to determine what resistor you want to use. I was wondering about diode use...are you wiring in series or parallel(sp?)? I would recommend parallel, since this is for brake light purposes. <P>(Just read your first post, you're already using parallel).<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 05-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||05-03-2001||01:20 PM||||I'm using strings of 7 diodes in series, and then wiring the series strings in parallel. I just wired up a 230 ohm resistor in series with the strings and it seems to be OK, however my power supply is just shy of 12 volts, and the battery on the bike will be around 13 or 14 volts at full charge, so I'll have to adjust that I guess to maybe 330 ohms or so. <BR>Still open to suggestions though...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||05-03-2001||02:30 PM||||If the battery on the bike is giving 13.8V (which would be normal) and the nominal current through the LED is 15mA then you need:<P>(13.8V - 11.9V)/0.015 = 126 Ohms resistor.<P>Since a 126 Ohm resistor does not exist, take something that's closest to it (ofcourse you coulf get 126 Ohms by using a few different value resistors, but it's not important to get the exact value).||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||05-03-2001||02:31 PM||||I forgot to say the resistor should be in series with the LEDs.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Taijiguy||05-03-2001||03:26 PM||||Thanks Cliff....I had a feeling that was going to be about it. I had some 230 ohm on hand, and wired them in place. Let it burn for a couple of hours, and had no excessive heat or degredation of the LED's so, I guess I got it. Thanks for the help  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierohoho||05-04-2001||06:34 PM||||Just curious, are you using the new super bright LED's or the regular ones. If the superbrights, where did you get them and how much? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Taijiguy||05-04-2001||08:38 PM||||For this project I'm only using standard LED's, but when I do this for mine, I'll use the 3000 mCD lights. Both types plus a bunch of others are available at <A HREF="http://www.mpja.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mpja.com/</A>  <BR>The standard kind I got for $3.50/100 and the 3000 mCD start at .29/ea and drop off down to .20/ea in quantity. They're pretty good to deal with, on-line ordering, and the items I ordered were delivered in a couple of days. And they alot of cool stuff on their site.<BR>~Taiji<BR>BTW I finished it last night, looks cool as hell. I'll try to post pics in a few days if I can get some. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||05-04-2001||11:34 PM||||also look for mirror turn signals in the archives.... we've been over some of this before. It might help.<P>For others... No, I haven't forgotten the mirror signals I was working on before I got sick. I just haven't had the time to worry about them. I do have the LED array figured out. I don't have the time to make up a shell with them. (It looks like I have to slightly alter a shell to keep clearance for the mirror glass to move properly.)||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierohoho||05-05-2001||05:48 PM||||taijiguy, thanks much for the info on the super brights. I haven't had much luck finding them till now. That web site should do the trick.||||reg||1
